HW6: Universal Machine (UM)
Karla Armoush and Eyad Soliman

- We believe that all parts of the assigment have been implemented correctly
- No major differences from the design document
- Program Architecture:

um.c (The program's main driver)
Abstraction: UM front end that owns the fetch-decode-execute loop.
Secrets: Holds the program counter, keeps a pointer to segment 0 for speed.
Relationships:
Client of memory for all segment access and of registers for register state.
Directs to operations after decoding each 32bit word with the Bitpack interface

memory.c/.h
Abstraction: A segmented-memory heap addressable by 32bit IDs plus word offsets
Secrets: 
        - Internally stores all segments in a Seq_T sequence of UArray_T blocks
        with a second Seq_T acting as a tracker of recycled IDs.
        - Segment 0 is always mapped.
        - IDs are recycled.
        - All allocation/deallocation details are hidden.
Relationships:
Serves um.c (initial program load) and serves operations for some of the memory
operations such as map/unmap, segmented store and load.. etc.
Never touches registers directly.

registers.c/.h
Abstraction: Eight 32-bit registers addressed 0-7.
Secrets: 
        - Implemented as a plain uint32_t regs[8].
        - Zero-initialized once at the beginning of the machine cycle.
Relationships:
Serves operations for every instruction.

operations.c/.h
Abstraction: Functions that implement the 14 UM opcodes provided in the spec.
Secrets: Internally handles all of the instructions.
Relationships:
um.c uses operations.c after decoding.
Clients: 
        - Calls memory and registers to observe or change machine state.
        - Never touches the fetch loop.
        
Note that we are also using the CS40 provided bitpack.c.A at the
decode stage (used by um.c).
Other external libraries used include Hanson Seq_T and UArray_T

Time to perform 50 Million Instructions

    We estimate our program to take ~2 seconds to perform 50 million
    UM instructions. We tested this using midmark.um (which has ~80 million
    instructions according to the lab10 spec) and the cputiming interface
    that we were provided with back during the assignment locality. Before
    beginning the program loop, we kept a counter that began at 0 for the
    number of instructions executed, and when that reached 50 million,
    we had it stop the timer and print the time it took perform them. The
    cputiming interface generally gave us values between 1.8-2.2 seconds.

    Note: when using the -O3 compiler flag, we were getting times in the
    range 1.1-1.4 seconds on average

Unit Tests Used: We tested using the provided lab testing framework

1) halt_test: 
calls the HALT instruction once and confirms that the UM stops
immediately when opcode 7 is executed
2) LV_and_output_test:
uses LV to load the bytes H e l l o 40 \n into registers and outputs them using
the OUTPUT instruction
3) io_test:
Reads 8 bytes from stdin (using successive INPUT calls) and then ouputs them 
using the OUTPUT instruction. 
4) special_io_test:
Same io purpose as the test above, but intended to test inputting and 
outputting
non-printable ASCII characters.
5) arithmetic_test: 
tests the addition, multiplication and division instructions
to compute the ASCII characters for "c" "a" "t" and the newline character.
6) divide_truncate test:
divides 100 by 3 and outputs 33 demonstration floor division.
7) nand_test: 
reads one byte, NANDs it with 0x0F, NANDs with self to isolate the lower 4 bits
and adds 48 to turn it into a readable ASCII digit before ouput
8) cmov_zero_test:
executes CMOV with condition register = 0, verifies it behaves as a no op.
9) cmov_mov_non_zero_test:
executes CMOV with condition register not equal to 0, verifies the move occurs.
10) load_progam_test:
uses LOADP with rb = 0 to stay in segment 0 but jump PC forward, skipping three
HALTs.
11) map_unmap_test:
tests MAP, UNMAP, and ID recycling logic.
maps segments of sizes 50 and 100 (IDs 1 and 2), unmap ID 1, maps again to show
the ID is recycled, and prints the IDs.
12) sstore_sload_test:
maps a segment, SSTOREs the byte 'a' at offset 4, then SLOADs it back and 
outputs it. validates segment memory write/read operations.
13) load_segment_test:
builds a small segment containing an encoded HALT, stores it into word 4, 
then LOADPs that segment and jumps to word 4, confirms deep-copy duplication of
a non-zero segment and correct PC reset.